A patient presents with the following case:


A 34-year-old male presents to the hematology clinic for evaluation of prolonged bleeding after a recent dental extraction, which required packing and suturing to achieve hemostasis. He reports a history of easy bruising since childhood and one episode of prolonged bleeding after a tonsillectomy at age 8. He denies joint swelling, hematuria, or family history of bleeding disorders. He takes no anticoagulant medications.

Initial laboratory workup reveals a prothrombin time (PT) of 12.8 seconds (normal: 11–13.5 seconds) and an activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) of 58 seconds (normal: 25–35 seconds). Complete blood count, platelet count (245,000/μL), and fibrinogen level (310 mg/dL) are within normal limits. Thrombin time is 15 seconds (normal: 14–19 seconds).

A 1:1 mixing study is performed, and the aPTT corrects to 31 seconds, falling well within the normal range, indicating full normalization upon mixing with normal pooled plasma.

