A patient presents with the following case:


A 58-year-old male presents to his primary care physician with a persistent cough and intermittent dyspnea on exertion over the past six months. He has a 15-pack-year smoking history but quit eight years ago. He reports occasional wheezing during cold weather and morning chest tightness that sometimes limits his ability to walk uphill. His symptoms have been progressively bothersome, with an mMRC dyspnea score of 2.

On examination, he appears well-nourished with no acute distress. Lung auscultation reveals mildly diminished breath sounds bilaterally with scattered expiratory wheezes. Oxygen saturation is 96% on room air. Spirometry is performed: pre-bronchodilator FEV1 is 1.82 L (62% predicted) and FVC is 2.55 L (72% predicted), yielding an FEV1/FVC ratio of 0.71. Despite the preserved ratio, his FEV1 is notably reduced, and his symptom burden remains significant, raising concern for possible air trapping or volume response.

