A 38-year-old woman presents with progressive dyspnea on exertion over the past 18 months. She reports no history of seizures, intellectual disability, or skin lesions. Physical examination reveals decreased breath sounds bilaterally. There are no facial angiofibromas, ungual fibromas, or hypomelanotic macules. High-resolution CT of the chest demonstrates numerous thin-walled cysts distributed diffusely throughout both lung fields, consistent with a cystic lung disease pattern. Abdominal CT with contrast shows no renal masses and no evidence of angiomyolipoma. There is no lymphangioleiomyoma identified, and no chylous pleural or peritoneal effusions are present. She has no clinical features meeting criteria for tuberous sclerosis complex. Given the imaging findings and clinical suspicion, serum VEGF-D level is obtained and returns at 1,240 pg/mL. Pulmonary function testing reveals an FEV1 of 68% predicted with a reduced DLCO of 52% predicted. She is a never-smoker with no relevant occupational exposures.

